    "narrative": "The heart of our lineup today features **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) and **James Wood** (73.10), who anchor the unit with the highest projected outputs. **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits significantly from a plus-park environment against Mitch Keller, pairing a 10% park boost with a favorable platoon split. Meanwhile, **James Wood** remains a must-start despite the presence of Wacha; his elite 0.407 xwOBA and high-leverage stolen base potential allow him to maintain a stellar score even when factoring in a 2% penalty for the opposing battery\u2019s hold capability. **Josh Naylor** (71.79) also earns a premium spot, as he faces Brandon Young in a favorable matchup that maximizes his current power profile.\n\nOur deepest roster dilemma centered on the final utility and outfield spots. **Travis Bazzana** (45.58) narrowly missed the cut; while his 19% SP skill boost against Robert Gasser is enticing, the forced L/L platoon disadvantage ultimately suppressed his efficiency score below our threshold for starting. Similarly, we are exercising caution with **Corbin Carroll** (51.79); while his speed is a perennial asset, he faces a tough assignment against Reid Detmers, and the inclusion of an elite defensive catcher who limits stolen base success creates a downward drag on his final projection. \n\nWe are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for most of our starters, particularly **Dalton Rushing** (40.62) and **Gabriel Moreno** (43.68), both of whom hover near our efficiency floor. The risk of the #8 spot for **Dalton Rushing** against the disciplined Drew Rasmussen is a notable concern, as is the potential for **Max Muncy** (59.00) to lose plate appearances if the late-order spot sticks. We are tracking these rotations closely, as any shift from our assumed lineup positions\u2014specifically for **Cole Young** at lead-off or **Henry Bolte** at the bottom of the order\u2014could necessitate immediate benching to preserve our season-long game caps.",
